{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>When you build a new house, etc. That the faller fall from it.</b> From the language of our Rabbis of blessed memory (Shabbat 32a) — leave him, for he is one who throws himself down — thus the word 'from it' (מִמֶּנּוּ) refers back to 'the faller,' and this was said so that you should not say: but does the parapet annul the decree, when this one is worthy of falling? Therefore it says that this is not so, for you shall not bring blood upon your house, for one whose blood is on him and who is worthy of falling — why should he be in your house? And in any case, one worthy of falling will not be saved by the parapet, for 'the faller shall fall from it' — he falls of himself, even on level ground. But if you do not make a parapet, then he falls from you and not from himself, and HaShem wanted him himself to cause his own fall, for it was from his own hand that this came to him. And there are those who say: 'from it' refers back to the parapet, for one worthy of falling will fall even from the parapet, and the parapet saves only one who is not worthy of falling." "And our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Tanhuma, Shofetim 1): if you fulfill the commandment of the parapet, you will merit a vineyard. What has the parapet to do with the vineyard? But because wine causes the drinker to fall, as is brought in the Yalkut: what is the vine? One prunes it and it is silent, and afterward it attacks a man and makes him fall, etc. — see the end of parashat Vayeshev (Yalkut Shimoni, Vayeshev 146); therefore, specifically after making the parapet that saves from falling, you will merit a vineyard, and not before then. For 'that the faller fall' (כִּי יִפֹּל הַנֹּפֵל) is a hint to this: the final letters of the words כי יפול הנופל add up to the numerical value of יַיִן (wine). And know that the passage of sending the mother bird begins with כ and ends with ם — that is, between them all: סַ״ךְ (samekh-kaf). And so the passage of the parapet and the vineyard begins with כ and ends with ם, after the manner of 'סוֹמֵךְ ה׳ לְכָל הַנֹּפְלִים' (Psalms 145:14). For in the sending of the mother bird, if his father said to him: go up to the dovecote and bring me fledglings, and he went up and fell and died — where is the lengthening of his days? But HaShem supports, to grant him length of days for a world that is entirely long (Chullin 142a). And in the making of the parapet, He supports all those who fall, who are worthy of falling. And in 'you shall not sow your vineyard with mixed species' — where one is not liable until he sows wheat and barley and grape seed with a single hand-sowing (Berakhot 22a) — and through this he causes for himself 'it shall be burned with fire' (Kiddushin 56b). But when he does not sow in this manner, HaShem supports all those who fall by a hand-sowing. And close to this is what R. Isaac Ba'al ha-Turim explained. And from within it you will see what I have added from my own."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {1 "The acrostic reading depends on identifying the first and last letters of specific Torah passages in Deut. 22:6–12; 'סַ״ךְ' spelled samekh-kaf is interpreted as a signpost for 'סוֹמֵךְ' (supports). The phrase 'בַּמַפּוֹלֶת יָד' appears both as a technical halakhic term for sowing by hand (Berakhot 22a) and is then reread as 'those who fall by a hand-sowing' — this double sense is preserved in the translation."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:13:23.896868589Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}